Mutex example
=============

Shows the ARINC 653 mutex services (Part 1, added in Supplement 4) in a
partition with several processes that share data:

  CREATE_MUTEX             creates a mutex during initialization, with a
                           priority and a queuing discipline (FIFO or
                           PRIORITY) for waiting processes. The number of
                           mutexes is set by <ARINC653_Mutexes Count="..."/>
                           in the partition's config.xml.
  GET_MUTEX_ID             finds a mutex by name (case insensitive).
  ACQUIRE_MUTEX            takes the mutex; if another process owns it,
                           waits up to TIME_OUT (0: don't wait,
                           INFINITE_TIME_VALUE: wait as long as needed).
  RELEASE_MUTEX            releases it; the first waiting process becomes
                           the owner.
  GET_MUTEX_STATUS         owner, state, priority, lock count and number of
                           waiting processes.
  GET_PROCESS_MUTEX_STATE  which mutex a process owns.
  RESET_MUTEX              releases a mutex owned by another process, e.g.
                           one that failed while owning it.

Priority ceiling: the priority given to CREATE_MUTEX is the mutex's
ceiling. The owner runs at that priority until it releases the mutex, then
gets its own priority back, so no process with a priority up to the
ceiling can preempt it while it uses the shared data. A process with a
higher priority than the ceiling can't acquire the mutex (INVALID_MODE).
A process owns at most one mutex at a time; it may acquire it again (the
lock count goes up) and must release it as many times.

What P1 does
------------

P1 shares a navigation data record (update number, altitude, valid flag)
between four processes, protected by the mutex NAV_DATA with priority 20
and PRIORITY queuing. Each process waits until a fixed time after the
start of NORMAL mode, so the run is the same every time:

  writer (12)   updates the record. At 0 ms it acquires NAV_DATA (running
                at priority 20) and holds it for 100 ms; at 500 ms it
                writes a second update.
  display (14)  reads the record. At 20 ms it waits for NAV_DATA and gets
                it when writer releases it; at about 320 ms it waits again.
  monitor (10)  at 40 ms shows the mutex status (owned by writer, one
                waiting process), tries ACQUIRE_MUTEX with TIME_OUT 0
                (NOT_AVAILABLE) and 20 ms (TIMED_OUT); at 150 ms the mutex
                is free and it gets it at once.
  faulty (13)   at 300 ms acquires NAV_DATA, starts an update (the record
                is marked invalid) and at 350 ms fails with
                RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR, while it still owns the mutex.

The error handler gets the error, finds the mutex with
GET_PROCESS_MUTEX_STATE, stops faulty and calls RESET_MUTEX. The waiting
display process becomes the owner and sees that the update didn't finish.
Without RESET_MUTEX, display would wait forever: stopping a process
doesn't release its mutex.

During initialization, main also shows that a second CREATE_MUTEX fails
with INVALID_CONFIG, because the configuration allows one mutex.

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory:

    make mutex                      # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu mutex       # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel examples/mutex/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel examples/mutex/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

Quit QEMU with Ctrl-A X.

Expected output (x86_64). The numbers in brackets are milliseconds since
NORMAL mode started, rounded to 10 ms; they may differ by 10 ms or so,
for example on arm64.

    P1: CREATE_MUTEX(NAV_DATA, priority 20, PRIORITY): NO_ERROR
    P1: CREATE_MUTEX(SECOND): INVALID_CONFIG (Count is 1)
    P1: SET_PARTITION_MODE(NORMAL)
    [   0] writer: ACQUIRE_MUTEX: NO_ERROR, priority 20 (base 12)
    [  20] display: ACQUIRE_MUTEX, waiting...
    [  40] monitor: NAV_DATA is OWNED, owner writer, lock count 1, 1 waiting
    [  40] monitor: ACQUIRE_MUTEX, TIME_OUT 0: NOT_AVAILABLE
    [  60] monitor: ACQUIRE_MUTEX, TIME_OUT 20 ms: TIMED_OUT
    [ 100] writer: wrote update #1, RELEASE_MUTEX
    [ 100] display: ACQUIRE_MUTEX: NO_ERROR, priority 20 (base 14)
    [ 100] display: update #1, altitude 10500 ft
    [ 100] writer: priority 12 again
    [ 150] monitor: ACQUIRE_MUTEX, TIME_OUT 0: NO_ERROR, update #1
    [ 300] faulty: ACQUIRE_MUTEX: NO_ERROR, starting update #2
    [ 330] display: ACQUIRE_MUTEX, waiting...
    [ 350] faulty: RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR("update aborted")
    [ 350] error handler: error in faulty: "update aborted"
    [ 350] error handler: STOP: NO_ERROR
    [ 350] error handler: it owns NAV_DATA (GET_PROCESS_MUTEX_STATE)
    [ 350] error handler: NAV_DATA is OWNED, owner faulty, lock count 1, 1 waiting
    [ 360] error handler: RESET_MUTEX: NO_ERROR
    [ 360] error handler: NAV_DATA is OWNED, owner display, lock count 1, 0 waiting
    [ 360] display: ACQUIRE_MUTEX: NO_ERROR, priority 20 (base 14)
    [ 360] display: data invalid (update #2 didn't finish)
    [ 500] writer: ACQUIRE_MUTEX: NO_ERROR, priority 20 (base 12)
    [ 500] writer: wrote update #2, RELEASE_MUTEX
    [ 500] writer: priority 12 again
    [ 500] writer: NAV_DATA is AVAILABLE, owner nobody, lock count 0, 0 waiting
    [ 500] writer: done

Note the order at 100 ms: writer releases NAV_DATA, and display, now the
owner at priority 20, runs before writer can print its next line.
